A THROWAWAY self-signed certificate and its private key, for one test.

tests/test_sources_tls.py starts a TLS server on 127.0.0.1 with this pair and
checks that the relaxed context (Kaohsiung's and Hsinchu's,
`x509_strict=False`) still REFUSES it -- i.e. that clearing
ssl.VERIFY_X509_STRICT did not turn into "trust everything". Proving a
negative about certificate verification needs a certificate the client must
reject, and this is it.

This key is public, committed on purpose, and worthless: it signs nothing but
a localhost socket inside a test, it is in no trust store anywhere, and CN is
"localhost". It is the same arrangement CPython's own ssl tests use
(Lib/test/certdata/keycert.pem). Do not reuse it for anything.

  Subject/Issuer  CN=localhost (self-signed)
  SAN             DNS:localhost, IP:127.0.0.1
  Generated       2026-09-17, openssl 3.5.3, RSA 2048
  Valid until     Aug 24 00:23:57 2126 GMT -- deliberately a century out, so
                  this file never becomes a mystery test failure.

